        "editor_spans": [
          {
            "body": "In the full speech, the Prime Minister's line that Scotland is 'one of the most centralised nations in Europe' leaves out some history. The regions he now wants to 'power up' used to exist. From 1975 to 1996, Scotland had regional councils, and Strathclyde covered Motherwell, Glasgow and the Ravenscraig site where he spoke. Westminster abolished them in 1996, before there was a Scottish Parliament, and replaced them with 32 single-tier councils. Holyrood inherited that flattened structure rather than creating it. The 'City Region Deals' he mentions are partly a later patch over the gap. So his story that a London centre ignored the regions while Edinburgh has kept power since 1999 is only half the account. Scotland's missing regional tier was removed from London, and the 'devolution by default' now on offer would in part rebuild what Whitehall took apart.",
            "cites": [
              153,
              271,
              274,
              352,
              80
            ]
          },
          {
            "body": "The most important argument in the speech is not the offer itself. It is how the speech reframes the divide. 'We spend a lot of time focusing on the differences between Scotland and England. But what is perhaps more striking is how similar the problems' are. That moves Scottish politics from a nation-against-nation axis, which has favoured the SNP for two decades, to a centre-against-places axis, with Holyrood counted as one of the centres. Ravenscraig and Leigh are put side by side as the same injury done by the same kind of distant decision-maker. If the frame holds, the case for independence, which rests on Westminster being the problem, loses force, because Edinburgh becomes part of the problem too. This builds on what we said before about Westminster setting itself up as the champion of Scotland's towns against the Scottish Government. Behind the 'outstretched hand' is a claim about what Scottish grievance is really about.",
            "cites": [
              125,
              126,
              136,
              96,
              84,
              366
            ]
          },
          {
            "body": "The concrete offer is 'DWP devolution', and it comes with conditions. Employment support would be 'shaped by Holyrood', but in the same breath it 'has to be based on the Easterhouse principle' and must go 'right down', or 'it won't work'. Innovation money is also meant to go to 'Scotland's regions', not to the Scottish Government. So Westminster is handing over powers it controls, but only on terms that require Holyrood to pass them down to local level. Readers should also know this is not new ground. The 2016 Scotland Act already devolved some employment programmes, and Holyrood's own 'No One Left Behind' approach already runs employability support through local council partnerships. The real test at the National Economic Council meeting is whether Jobcentre Plus and its core money, which are still reserved to Westminster, are actually on the table. If they are not, the offer is mostly a demand on Edinburgh presented as a gift.",
            "cites": [
              305,
              306,
              308,
              310,
              313,
              216,
              299,
              318
            ]
          },
          {
            "body": "The Easterhouse story is the most revealing part of the speech. It describes a project run by local people that worked because nobody designed it centrally. The Prime Minister then proposes to 'take the Easterhouse model nationwide' as 'the way we support everybody, everywhere'. That tension runs through every attempt to spread grassroots success: once a model is scaled up and made a condition of funding, it starts to become the top-down, tick-box system it was meant to replace. Live Well in Greater Manchester is his evidence that it can be spread, but no figures were offered. Before anyone judges 'names not numbers', it would be fair to ask what the numbers from Live Well actually show.",
            "cites": [
              51,
              56,
              61,
              315,
              316,
              57,
              238
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
